{"purpose":"Validate the team-collaboration theme as a new roadmap for the EKA ecosystem: per-project team isolation, knowledge sharing and discoverability, joint review/approval workflows, multi-user identity and permissions, and work item assignment with member-scoped visibility — so EKA supports teams working on projects, not only single operators.","content":"Theme under validation: EKA team collaboration. The theme targets every EKA project (eka, nest-desacorp, future adopters), not eka tooling specifically — EKA gains the capability to isolate teams per project. Candidate capability areas identified in the planning discussion: (1) sharing and discoverability of knowledge across team members; (2) joint review and approval workflows with a durable review trail; (3) multi-user workspace with per-member identity and permissions; (4) cross-project coordination inside an organization; (5) assignment of work items to team members, where each member sees and executes only the items assigned to them. Research questions to answer: what collaboration model fits the existing synchronization/immutability architecture (ADR-010, ADR-011, ADR-027); how identity and permissions map onto the repository-context model (ADR-017, ADR-018); how assignment and member-scoped visibility integrate with the work item/ticket/gate model (ADR-019); and what the minimal viable slice is before any planning commitment. Outcome of the discussion: the theme is validated and locked to the minimal slice 'Assigned Work' (assignment as data + member-scoped views, no authorization) as the first requirement; subsequent phases are lightweight joint review/approval (per-reviewer trail, no PR-style blockers), soft team isolation (data + application-layer enforcement), and deferred candidates (sharing/discoverability, cross-project coordination). AI agents act on behalf of the operator, not as separate members.","investigationSummary":"Conflict/duplicate check performed against in-force knowledge (2026-08-14): no existing vision, strategy, requirement, ADR, scope, or plan covers team collaboration. Closest artifacts are ADR-019 (work item transitions, notes, gates — per-work-item review), ADR-027 (source-only snapshot transport — knowledge travels via git, no server), ADR-017/018 (repository identity and context), and ADR-010/013 (synchronization and store-backed projections). scp:eka-improvements explicitly defers FEEDBACK_ISSUE_TOKEN security hardening to a 'future roadmap', leaving room for a next roadmap unit. plan:eka-improvements is immutable and active; plan:plugin-install-trust is approved with a planned container — neither is contradicted by this theme. No fnd family knowledge exists yet in the eka namespace (first research finding). Architecture analysis (alex-architect, 2026-08-14): theme compatible with ADR-019 D2 via a three-layer model — engine carries data (member unit, assigned-to relationship, additive conditional R-rule, advisory filter projections), the application layer carries authority (authN/authZ), and org-level coordination uses a coordination-repo pattern without a new server; identity/namespace grammar untouched (ADR-001/017); all standard changes additive, following the ADR-019 D3/D5 precedent (cmt token, discusses relationship). Product analysis (althea-product-specialist, 2026-08-14): core problem is the missing people model (single-operator assumptions) with concrete evidence at nest-desacorp and fbk #3; priority ordering assignment+visibility, review/approval, identity (attribution now, authorization later), sharing, cross-project (deferred); MVP slice 'Assigned Work' keeps the theme small; plugin-install-trust is not preempted. Review (alex-qa PASS; alex-reviewer and althea-review-specialist PASS WITH FINDINGS, all addressed in the conclusion): sto:fix-author-consistency is done (slice 1 builds on it); R-rule must be conditional to keep changes additive; unassigned-item semantics added as open research item; 'enforcement' wording replaced by advisory filters; agent-on-behalf risk noted. Discussion evidence: cmt:team-collaboration-implementation (role implementation, author Marij Mokoginta).","conclusion":"VALIDATED, scoped to a minimal slice. The theme is real (evidence: ADR-027 was designed around team merges; nest-desacorp review notes aggregate multiple reviewer verdicts into single-author notes; fbk #3 documents author-identity inconsistency; sto:fix-author-consistency has shipped (done), showing the identity layer is being hardened) and compatible with the architecture in force on one condition: the engine adds data only (member unit, assigned-to relationship, advisory filters, review trail), while authority (authN/authZ, approval policy) lives in the application layer — honoring ADR-019 D2 ('no authentication in the engine') unchanged. The 'all changes additive' claim holds only if the phase-1 R-rule is conditional (binding only units that carry the new attribute; leaving existing data without assignee untouched) — the additive precedent is the token/relationship pattern (cmt, discusses per ADR-019 D3/D5), not the breaking R13 rule pattern. Soft team isolation is a collaborative convenience, not a security boundary; member-scoped views are advisory client-side filters until an authorization phase exists. Roadmap: slice 1 'Assigned Work' (assignee as data on work items, member-scoped view filters, built on the shipped sto:fix-author-consistency — no auth) becomes the first requirement; phase 2 adds lightweight joint review/approval (per-reviewer review trail via existing cmt/rvw, no GitHub-PR-style blockers); AI agents act on behalf of the operator (git identity), not as separate members — accepted decision, with the noted risk that the audit trail cannot distinguish agent from operator and agent scope equals operator scope; sharing/discoverability and cross-project coordination are deferred candidates, not promises; FEEDBACK_ISSUE_TOKEN hardening is deferred (plan:eka-improvements outOfScope) with a roadmap slot open, not scheduled. Execution order: plugin-install-trust runs first; the team-collaboration requirement is prepared as the next roadmap unit. Open research items for the requirement phase: mbr- provenance (repo vs workspace level); board semantics of 'assigned' (container vs member) and the semantics of unassigned work items in member-scoped views; conditional R-rule nature and its impact on existing data; member identity representation and matching with the --by/git identity; and the attribute-vs-relationship choice for assignee (terminology to be unified at requirement time)."}